david there's a lot of discussion about  income inequality  if we accept that it's a problem  how can we actually fix it okay this is  a good question  um let's not debate whether income  inequality is a problem  let's accept that it is and that's not  what this segment is about  we can talk about whether it's a problem  in another segment i believe that it is  and i've outlined why  many times the most important  fact to realize about fixing income  inequality  is that we don't need to come up with  anything new  we we have essentially all the ideas we  need  to deal with income inequality now to be  very clear  i don't believe that we need perfect  distribution of income  perfect distribution equality of  outcomes so to speak  is not naturally occurring anywhere i  don't believe it would be good to force  it  i'm not a socialist i don't believe  there would be any reasonable  reasonable expectation that everybody  has the same amount of money what i do  think as a social democrat though  is the bottom should be lifted there  should be a safety net  that says we're not going to allow  people to fall below this level  and then at the top you should have more  restrictions about okay  if we say billionaires are okay are  multi-billionaires turbo billionaires  what at what point do we want to be  saying now is when you've got to really  be redistributing it's a gray area  different people would put it at a  different  different spot we have all of the  different  tools but the problem is getting the  right combination so as an example  should the minimum wage be higher than  it is right now  of course is a 15 minimum wage  nationally going to solve the problem of  inequality  no we need a higher minimum wage exactly  what it should be probably depends on  the state that we're in  but a 15 minimum wage isn't even a  living wage in many states based on cost  of living so  do we need to raise the minimum wage yes  there will be people who come in and say  well  if a higher minimum wage is better why  not just make it 200  an hour that's not an equilibrium wage  anywhere  and that's a red herring don't fall for  it do we need a more robust social  service system  funded by taxes on the very very rich  but that includes financial transactions  and some of these other ideas yeah we've  got the tools we know what it would look  like  there's just no political will to do it  right now  do we need better educational access  regardless of income because one of the  great equalizers is  education and unfortunately in our  society  yes there are scholarships yes we know  about all that stuff  but the reality is um there is very low  income mobility in the united states and  part of that is because there is not  widespread  good education that is accessible to  people who start off in an economically  disadvantaged position  trade is part of it you know trump talks  about trade and some trade things can be  bad for the middle class  i'll concede that you've got to look at  trade we can look at things like  universal basic income or  a value-added tax and there's pros and  cons to all of these  my point is there are no new tools that  are necessary to deal with income  inequality  but we have to get away from do nothing  and let the markets handle it  because we know that doesn't work we  know that doesn't work  i'm fine as a social democrat letting  markets work to direct resources  in many industries as i've said before i  don't need the government  making all the cell phones or making all  the airplanes but  i sure as hell want the government  involved in regulating those industries  and well-regulated markets where private  industry is manufacturing stuff  i'm fine with certain areas need to be  taken out of that healthcare  education you could argue that some  basic level of  housing should be taken out of that it's  a debate in the gray area but we've got  all the tools  we need to figure out how to put them  together in the right proportions  send in your questions for next week's  audience questions through  our website davidpakman.com we have a  great bonus show for you today and  remember that on august 23rd  the 15th anniversary of the david pakman  show  we will be doing a one day huge  membership special it will be based on  the theme of 15 years  and you can be notified of it by jumping  on our free newsletter  at davidpakman.com
 

